        {
          "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
          "ShowInDocs": true,
          "Name": "Burdekin Sowing Time Experiment 2009",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "DID I CONVERT THE DATA TO M2?",
              "Name": "Is observed data correct check perry obs files",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "29/01/2024:\n\nCheck soils and compare with APSIM Classic sims\n\n===================================================================================\n\n**25/01/2024**\n\n**From: C:\\Users\\rhe005\\OneDrive - CSIRO\\Work Files\\00. From Desktop 20221019\\Modelling report --> Modelling results.xlxs, Tab \"Queensland - Burdekin\"**\n\n\n1. 2008 most problems - weakest dataset\n2. Remove sites with boll rots\n3. Need dataset with abortions\n4. Planting 3 easist to validate - no cloudy weather\n5. ***Good sites: 2008, PL3 and 2009 PL3***\n6. ***2012 cloudy --> high LAI***\n7. ***Cloudy years most important one --> highest deviation***\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\n===================================================================================\n\n1/12/2023:\n\n\n**NEED TO BE ABLE TO MODEL RE-GROWTH**\n\n1. cloudiness-related fruit loss and subsequent canopy responses changed the ratio of reproductive vs vegetative biomass partitioning.\n\n2. Differences in biomass partitioning between cultivars were minor. When cloud cover abated, extended\nconcurrent vegetative and reproductive growth enabled yield recovery. Greater LAI was a consequence of\ncompensatory processes following fruit loss. Faster development of LAI in response to the monsoon during early\nflowering could further shade lower canopy fruiting sites, exacerbating fruit abscission and impacting yield\ncompensation.\n\n\n3. Compare observed data with that presented in the papers: Table 3 Grundy et al FCR 2020 107868\n\n===================================================================================\n\n* Irrigation was scheduled using electronic capacitance probes(Envirotech, EP100EL-12) that were installed to a depth of 120 cm with dielectric sensors located at 10 cm intervals. \n\n* Two probes were installed in different replicates for each sowing time, enabling real time monitoring of soil moisture via an integrated radio frequency linked telemetry-based software system. \n\n* Irrigation was applied using ground water, scheduled at a 35–45 mm water deficit in the top 50 cm of the soil profile and refilled to the drained upper limit during each irrigation.\n\n* ***The crops were sown with a cone seeder on 750 mm equidistant spaced rows on raised beds (two rows per bed) separated by surface irrigation furrows.***\n\n====================================================================================\n\nSee papers:\n\nCotton production during the tropical monsoon season. I – The influence of variable radiation on boll loss, compensation and yield\n\nP. R. Grundy, S. J. Yeates and K. L. Bell\n\nField Crops Research 2020 Vol. 254 Pages 107790\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.fcr.2020.107790\n\n\nCotton production during the tropical monsoon season. II – Biomass accumulation, partitioning and RUE in response to boll loss and compensation\n\nP. R. Grundy, S. J. Yeates and K. L. Bell\n\nField Crops Research 2020 Vol. 255 Pages 107868\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.fcr.2020.107868\n\n\n\n",
              "Name": "Memo",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "\n1/12/2023: See Fig. 5 Grundy, Yeates, Bell. 2020 Cotton production during the tropical monsoon season. 1.\n\n\n\nStage|P1|DAS|P2|DAS|P3|DAS\n-|-|-|-|-|-|-\nSowing|1 Dec 2008|0|19 Dec 2008|0|7 Jan 2009|0\nEmergence||\nSquaring|22 Dec 2008|21|11 Jan 2009|23|2 Feb 2009|26\nFlowering|12 Jan 2009|42|5 Feb 2009|48|5 Mar 2009|57\nCutout||\nOpenBolls|5 Mar 2009|94|19 Apr 2009|121|5 May 2009|118\nMaturity|14 May 2009|164|12 May 2009|144|10 Jun 2009|154\nDefoliation|5 May 2009?|155?|12 May 2009|144|12 Jun 2009|156\nHarvestRipe|24 May 2009|174|27 May 2009|159|30 Jun 2009|174\n",
              "Name": "Phenology stages Observed",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "\nFROM: \n\nGrundy, P. R., Yeates, S. J., & Bell, K. L. (2020). \nCotton production during the tropical monsoon season. I – The influence of variable radiation on boll loss, compensation and yield. \nField Crops Research, 254, 107790. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fcr.2020.107790 \n\nPage 3:\n\n\"Mepiquat chloride (MC) was originally applied to all treatments \nusing rates and timing according to guidelines determined for MC usage \nat the Ord River in tropical Western Australia (Yeates et al., 2002, \n2007) but usage was modified in response to the early December \nsowing in Season 1 when MC rate appeared too high with reduced \nsympodia elongation in the upper canopy observed that may have \ncompromised compensation capacity. A more precautionary approach \nwas used for all other treatments and MC was applied at the lower end \nof the recommended rate range (7.6–19 g ai ha−1) from sixth node \ngrowth stage onwards.\"",
              "Name": "Growth regulator details",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
